  "query_preview": "/SATURDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 256 AM MDT Wed Oct 14 2026\n\nSynoptically, we find ourselves in northwest flow aloft on Saturday \nas a ridge develops over the eastern Rockies/northern Plains. The \nupper-level pattern will briefly shift westerly then \nsouthwesterly flow Sunday as this system propagates eastward \nacross the north- central CONUS. Winds aloft should remain from \nthe southwest until a shortwave passage shifts them to be \nbriefly zonal or west- northwestward on Monday. After this, \nensemble guidance suggests more ridging building in Tuesday \nwhich insinuates southwest upper-level flow once again. \nDescending to the surface, high pressure is forecasted Saturday \nthrough late Sunday. A weak low, associated with the shortwave, \nshould then move across the County Warning Area (CWA) Monday. No\nfrontal boundaries are anticipated with the overall system over\nthe four-day forecast period, therefore limiting any \nprecipitation chances to shortwave passages.\n\nIt is likely to be dry Saturday through Tuesday as current \nProbability of Precipitation (PoP) guidance has 5% or less for the \nentire long-term period. High temperatures Saturday should be on the \nmore seasonable side, with general 60s and low 70s expected \nthroughout the CWA. Sunday/Monday should see a bit of a warmup \nas 70s across the board are anticipated. Said warmup should \nculminate in upper 70s for Tuesday. Low overnight temperatures \nare what we will have to watch for this timeframe as well. Many \nlocales have a strong chance to see their first frost/freeze of \nthe season this Saturday with low temperatures projected to \nbottom out in the low 30s. Guidance currently has a range of \n30-33 degrees for the entire CWA. Looking ahead, lows Sunday \nthrough Tuesday are expected to sit in the 40s in some locations\nand upper 30s in others, so freeze potential will be unlikely \nthose mornings.\n\nFire weather potential will continued to be monitored throughout the \nweek. Currently, there is a low chance of critical fire weather \nconditions being met in eastern Colorado on Saturday, according to \nguidance. Relative Humidity (RH) values possibly reaching 14/15% is \nin the mix on Saturday for areas west of US-385. Simultaneously, \nsoutherly surface winds are expected to peak around 20-25 mph, \ngusting 35-40 mph. For the rest of the forecast period, RH values \nreaching the upper teens is likely Sunday and Monday for our eastern \nColorado counties. Northerly winds, shifting southeast with the \nshortwave passage, generally 5-20 mph with gusts up to 30 mph is \nexpected. Tuesday's RHs and winds are not expected to be of concern \nfor fire weather in eastern Colorado. Elsewhere in the CWA, RH \nvalues in the 20-40% range is likely through the weekend and into \nnext week. General winds of 5-20 mph, gusting to 30 mph is\nexpected.",
